  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nAt 2 PM CDT, skies are partly to mostly cloudy with ongoing showers \nand thunderstorms scattered across the forecast area. A cold front \nhas moved into the western Dakotas, on its way toward Minnesota \nlater tonight. Temperatures are warming through the 70, with a few \nlocations warming into the 80s on south-southeast breezes 15 to 25 \nmph including gusts at times to 35-45 mph.\n\nThe upper level flow pattern, which has included an upper level \nridge parked over the northern plains for several days, is beginning \nto break down. Upper level longwave troffing looks to exert more \ninfluence than upper level ridging during the 7-day, knocking at \nleast a couple of categories of temperature off of high temps during \nmost of the forecast period.\n\nA cold front will pass over the CWA tonight, with a low level jet \nestablishing itself across eastern South Dakota prior to the fropa. \nExpect the given dynamic and thermodynamic set-up tonight ahead of \nthe front will either keep the faucet of current thunderstorms \nturned on well into this evening, or introduce an entirely separate \nperiod of thunderstorms late this evening across northeast South \nDakota on the nose of the low level jet as it develops. SPC still \nhas the eastern half of the CWA in a marginal risk for severe \nweather tonight. One inch hail and 60 mph damaging thunderstorm wind \ngusts are possible.\n\nBreezy/windy west-northwest winds and drier air on Tuesday will be \ncreating elevated (high to very high) fire danger conditions \nespecially across north central South Dakota. Criteria for a Red \nFlag Warning may night be reached Tuesday afternoon, but perhaps a \nSpecial Weather Statement might be useful to increase the visibility \nof Tuesday's fire weather situation.\n\nNear normal temperatures are forecast Tuesday night through \nWednesday night with the influence of an upper level trof north in \nthe region before things warm back up briefly Thursday through \nFriday when transient upper level ridging works quickly over the \nnorthern CONUS/southern Canada region.\n\nBeyond Friday, the pattern aloft over this CWA becomes influenced \nprimarily by upper level troffing (cooler than climo normal \nconditions?) that should bring a couple of meaningful precipitation \nchances to the CWA over the weekend into early next week.",
